# Event log

The event table is the source of truth. Tables for rules, skills, prompts, evaluations, and proposals are projections. Fix a projection by rebuilding it from events, not by editing history.

## Writes

- Insert only. No client `UPDATE` or `DELETE` policy on `events` or on projection tables.
- Unique `(stream_id, version)` for optimistic concurrency.
- Each event has `org_id`, `stream_id`, `stream_type`, `version`, `event_type`, `schema_version`, `payload`, `actor_id`, `occurred_at`.
- Postgres updates projections (trigger or function). Application code does not `UPDATE` projection tables.

## Closed stream types

`organization`, `membership`, `rule`, `skill`, `prompt`, `evaluation`, `proposal`

Name events in past-tense PascalCase (`RuleUpdated`) and set `schema_version`.

## Where a command runs

The web client may append an event only when RLS fully authorizes that write.

Branching logic, model calls, and commands that touch more than one stream go through `services/agent`. That service still only appends events.

```sql
-- ❌ projection is not the source of truth
update public.rules set body = '...' where id = '...';

-- ✅ append the fact; the projection follows
insert into public.events (
  org_id, stream_id, stream_type, version, event_type, schema_version, payload, actor_id
) values (
  '...', '...', 'rule', 2, 'RuleUpdated', 1, '{"body":"..."}', '...'
);
```

## Proposals

`ProposalCreated` → `ProposalVerified` → `ProposalApproved` or `ProposalRejected`.

The agent service may record `ProposalVerified`. Only a human member may record approval or rejection. Approval does not edit a projection by itself; the accepted change is a separate harness event.
